## Development

Node {{ node_version }}+ (`nvm use` reads `.nvmrc`); `npm install` refuses older versions.

```bash
npm install
npm run dev        # local Worker at http://localhost:8787
npm test           # vitest, running inside workerd against simulated bindings
npm run typecheck
uvx pre-commit install   # optional: run the lint stack on every commit
```

The top level of `wrangler.toml` is local development and tests only — it points at `-dev` resources that wrangler simulates on disk, so nothing you do locally touches deployed data. Deployed targets are the named environments (`[env.production]`{% if use_staging %}, `[env.staging]`{% endif %}).
{% if use_d1 %}

## Database

Migrations live in `migrations/` as numbered SQL files; tests apply them to the simulated database automatically (see `test/apply-migrations.ts`).

```bash
npm run migrations:local     # apply to the local dev database
{% if use_staging %}
npm run migrations:staging   # apply to the deployed staging database
{% endif %}
npm run migrations:prod      # apply to the deployed production database
```

The deploy pipeline applies migrations before each deploy, so the remote commands are rarely needed by hand.
{% endif %}
{% if use_r2 %}

## R2 CORS

R2 CORS rules live on the bucket, not in `wrangler.toml`. Edit `cors.json` and apply it per bucket:

```bash
npx wrangler r2 bucket cors set {{ project_name }} --file cors.json
{% if use_staging %}
npx wrangler r2 bucket cors set {{ project_name }}-staging --file cors.json
{% endif %}
```
{% endif %}
{% if use_cron %}

## Scheduled handler

The cron schedule is declared per environment in `wrangler.toml`. Exercise it locally with:

```bash
npx wrangler dev --test-scheduled
curl "http://localhost:8787/__scheduled?cron=0+3+*+*+*"
```
{% endif %}
{% if use_assets %}

## Static assets

Files under `public/` are served directly for matching paths; requests that match no file reach the Worker's `fetch()`. The `ASSETS` binding (`env.ASSETS.fetch(request)`) lets the Worker serve a file itself, for example as a single-page-app fallback. Asset routing is not exercised when tests call `worker.fetch()` directly; `test/worker.test.ts` fetches through the binding instead.
{% endif %}

## Deployment

Deploys run from CI on every push to main{% if use_staging %} — staging first, then production. Add required reviewers to the `production` GitHub environment to make that hand-off a manual approval gate{% endif %}. Two repository secrets are required:

- `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` — account-owned token with Workers Editor (product scope), plus Zone > Workers Routes > Write on the zone if the Worker has routes or a Custom Domain (needed when the route is added, changed or removed; a plain redeploy works with Editor alone){% if use_d1 %}, plus D1 Edit for the migration step{% endif +%}
- `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` — the Cloudflare account id

The first deploy of a new Worker needs Admin; run `npm run deploy` once from a logged-in shell, then CI redeploys.

Set a `HEALTH_URL` variable on each GitHub environment (the Worker's `/health` endpoint) to enable the post-deploy smoke test.

Manual deploys, if you must:

```bash
{% if use_staging %}
npm run deploy:staging
{% endif %}
npm run deploy   # production
```

Linting (Biome, [zizmor](https://docs.zizmor.sh/), actionlint, mdformat) runs via [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com); CI runs the same stack plus `tsc --noEmit` and vitest via the shared [`worker-ci`](https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/cloudflare-worker-template) reusable workflow.
